---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Sidebar from '../../components/Sidebar.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import Continue from '../../components/Continue.astro';
import { frameworkNav } from '../../lib/sections';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Framework"
  description="The UniDPP framework: six layers L0-L5, the fourteen invariants, the lens model, profile axes, capability classes S0-S3, the passive-to-active twin axis, and the degradation and outage doctrine."
  eyebrow="03 · How it works"
  sectioned
>
  <Sidebar slot="rail" heading="Framework" items={frameworkNav} />
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Six layers, fourteen invariants, four capability classes, one trust model. Every layer
    is an existing international standard or a published open system; the framework adds no
    proprietary layer.
  </p>

  <section id="architecture" class="plate" aria-labelledby="architecture-h">
    <h2 id="architecture-h">Architecture</h2>
    <p>
      One product, sold worldwide, is subject to many legal regimes simultaneously. A
      single physical passport therefore carries many jurisdiction, sector, and
      characteristic profiles at once — each independently governed, trusted, versioned,
      and served — without any region being the universal envelope. Precedents: the
      ePassport (ICAO) with per-state inspection and a CSCA master list; ISO 20022
      validation-rule profiles; DCAT-AP and its national profiles; UNTDED's historic
      regional subsets; ISO 10303 application modules; GS1 Digital Link resolver linksets;
      ETSI/eIDAS Trusted Lists.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      columns={['Layer', 'Content']}
      rows={[
        ['<strong>L0 — Identity (global)</strong>', 'One unique product identifier (ISO/IEC 15459 / EN 18219); a granularity model of model, batch, and item; one physical carrier.'],
        ['<strong>L1 — Neutral core passport</strong>', 'A minimal skeleton only: product ID, passport ID, status, timestamps, profile manifest, event log, signatures. Regional fields deliberately do not live in the base — EN 18223’s <code>contentSpecificationIds</code> is the European proto-version of the manifest, generalized here.'],
        ['<strong>L2 — Profiles</strong>', 'Registered, versioned objects composed on three axes (jurisdiction × sector × characteristic), each binding data points, transforms, cryptographic suites, trust requirements, access policies, presentation requirements, and a trigger predicate.'],
        ['<strong>L3 — Semantic registry</strong>', 'Every data point defined once in a FERIN register per ISO 19135, with governance roles and item lifecycle; profiles constrain and add, never redefine.'],
        ['<strong>L4 — Trust</strong>', 'Each profile’s attestations chain to its own jurisdiction’s trust framework; global verification via SIGNATIF cross-recognition (jurisdictional trust lists, a global master list, revocation propagation).'],
        ['<strong>L5 — Resolution and serving</strong>', 'Resolver linksets keyed by profile context, role, language, and request region — one carrier code, many destinations; regional service providers where data-residency law requires in-country serving.'],
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      Read the cutaway from the bottom: identity is the foundation everything stands on;
      resolution is the surface everyone meets. Each layer is governed and versioned
      independently.
    </p>
    <figure class="figure">
      <svg
        viewBox="0 0 760 580"
        role="img"
        aria-labelledby="how-cutaway-title how-cutaway-desc"
      >
        <title id="how-cutaway-title">The six layers of the framework, L0 at the base to L5 at the surface</title>
        <desc id="how-cutaway-desc">
          A vertical cutaway of six strata. From the bottom: L0 identity, one unique
          product identifier; L1 neutral core passport, a minimal skeleton; L2 profiles,
          jurisdiction, sector, and characteristic lenses; L3 semantic registry, every data
          point defined once; L4 trust, attestations chained to each jurisdiction's
          anchors; L5 resolution and serving, one carrier code with many destinations.
        </desc>

        <!-- L5 (top) -->
        <rect x="60" y="20" width="640" height="82" rx="4" class="stratum" />
        <text x="80" y="52" class="lcode">L5</text>
        <text x="130" y="52" class="lname">Resolution and serving</text>
        <text x="130" y="68" class="ldesc">One carrier code, many destinations: linksets keyed by<tspan x="130" dy="16">profile context, role, language, and request region.</tspan></text>

        <!-- L4 -->
        <rect x="60" y="112" width="640" height="82" rx="4" class="stratum" />
        <text x="80" y="144" class="lcode">L4</text>
        <text x="130" y="144" class="lname">Trust</text>
        <text x="130" y="160" class="ldesc">Attestations chain to each jurisdiction's anchors;<tspan x="130" dy="16">cross-recognition through SIGNATIF trust lists.</tspan></text>

        <!-- L3 -->
        <rect x="60" y="204" width="640" height="82" rx="4" class="stratum" />
        <text x="80" y="236" class="lcode">L3</text>
        <text x="130" y="236" class="lname">Semantic registry</text>
        <text x="130" y="260" class="ldesc">Every data point defined once, in a federated registry per ISO 19135 / FERIN.</text>

        <!-- L2 -->
        <rect x="60" y="296" width="640" height="82" rx="4" class="stratum lens-stratum" />
        <text x="80" y="328" class="lcode">L2</text>
        <text x="130" y="328" class="lname">Profiles</text>
        <text x="130" y="344" class="ldesc">Jurisdiction, sector, and characteristic lenses — each<tspan x="130" dy="16">binding data points, transforms, suites, and trust.</tspan></text>

        <!-- L1 -->
        <rect x="60" y="388" width="640" height="82" rx="4" class="stratum" />
        <text x="80" y="420" class="lcode">L1</text>
        <text x="130" y="420" class="lname">Neutral core passport</text>
        <text x="130" y="436" class="ldesc">A minimal skeleton only: product ID, passport ID,<tspan x="130" dy="16">status, manifest, event log, signatures.</tspan></text>

        <!-- L0 (base) -->
        <rect x="60" y="480" width="640" height="82" rx="4" class="stratum base" />
        <text x="80" y="512" class="lcode">L0</text>
        <text x="130" y="512" class="lname">Identity</text>
        <text x="130" y="528" class="ldesc">One unique product identifier (ISO/IEC 15459), with<tspan x="130" dy="16">bridges to national schemes; never re-minted.</tspan></text>

        <!-- Depth axis -->
        <line x1="30" y1="562" x2="30" y2="20" class="axis" />
        <polygon points="25,26 30,14 35,26" class="arrow-head" />
        <text x="20" y="300" transform="rotate(-90 20 300)" text-anchor="middle" class="axis-lbl">from surface to foundation</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        The layers are independently governed: L2 carries the tint of the lens model —
        the layer where jurisdictions meet the core.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="invariants" class="plate" aria-labelledby="invariants-h">
    <h2 id="invariants-h">The fourteen invariants</h2>
    <p>
      The framework is defined by fourteen invariants — statements that hold everywhere in
      the system, regardless of which profiles are mounted. Each is stated with its
      consequences here; the full normative statements are Part&nbsp;1 of the
      <a href="/specs/part-1-framework/">specification</a>.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      columns={['Invariant', 'Statement']}
      rows={[
        ['<strong>I1</strong> — One subject, one identity', 'Identifiers are minted once and never re-minted; dormant identifiers may be adopted later, never replaced.'],
        ['<strong>I2</strong> — Single definition, many constraints', 'Profiles constrain and transform; they never redefine. Disagreement between profiles is expected and auditable; contradiction within a provenance class is an integrity incident.'],
        ['<strong>I3</strong> — Finest recorded granularity', 'Inputs are recorded at the finest identifier granularity available, always; dormant identifiers are allowed.'],
        ['<strong>I4</strong> — Append-only event sourcing', 'The distributed, log-anchored event log is the authoritative record; no single party holds it alone.'],
        ['<strong>I5</strong> — Typed relationship algebra', 'Association, derivation, installation, type lineage, custody, and membership edges — with method, slot, pairing, alteration, recoverability, and visibility carried explicitly.'],
        ['<strong>I6</strong> — State machine legitimacy', 'Normative statuses with defined transitions and dated applicability, including retroactive; profile growth never changes identity.'],
        ['<strong>I7</strong> — Issuance where legal duty attaches', 'Issuance, hosting, identity, anchoring, archiving, and trust are separable duties; republication never transfers authority.'],
        ['<strong>I8</strong> — Passport as projection', 'The passport is a deterministic, registered, legally-cited projection of the testimonial record — testimonial by default, sensorial by exception; transforms publish uncertainty, rounding, and decision rules; no pre-transformed duplicates are stored.'],
        ['<strong>I9</strong> — Graded trust', 'Trust markers, multi-suite signatures, verdicts with coverage reports, three verification readings; revocation reason determines retroactivity; taint propagates through the graph.'],
        ['<strong>I10</strong> — Profiles on three axes', 'Jurisdiction × sector × characteristic, predicate-triggered; each profile binds data points, transforms, cryptographic suites, trust, access, presentation, and carriers.'],
        ['<strong>I11</strong> — No load-bearing proprietary layer', 'Mount, identity, semantics, and trust are open ISO/IEC/UN mechanisms; AAS is informative-only.'],
        ['<strong>I12</strong> — Enumeration resistance', 'The system is verifiable without being browsable; blind edges by default where households or sites would be revealed; proof-of-binding is not knowledge-of-parent; recall is predicate-based; logs anchor commitments, not facts.'],
        ['<strong>I13</strong> — Degradation ladder', 'Tier A offline minimum, Tier B served, Tier C archival; explicit freshness verdicts; availability decoupled from origin servers.'],
        ['<strong>I14</strong> — Continuity', 'Successions exist for hosts, authorities, schemes, algorithms, profiles, and regimes; identities outlive their issuers and hosts.'],
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      The <a href="/framework/standards/#seams">seams</a> — the twelve joints between
      layers — are tracked as first-class engineering surfaces, because every defect
      recorded in the audit of the EN series is a seam failure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="lens" class="plate" aria-labelledby="lens-h">
    <h2 id="lens-h">The lens model</h2>
    <p>
      A profile is a calibrated lens placed on the digital twin. The scene — the twin,
      invariant and append-only — is never altered by observation. The lens selects and
      transforms; it is versioned and registered.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      columns={['Lens property', 'Framework counterpart']}
      rows={[
        ['Scene', 'The digital twin: invariant, append-only; never altered by observation.'],
        ['Focal length', 'Granularity — zoom between model and instance.'],
        ['Depth of field', 'Traversal depth — the top levels in focus, the rest out of scope, not deleted.'],
        ['Colour filter', 'Classification transform — the same scene under jurisdictional tint; divergent classifications (“A” here, “B” there) are expected and published as decision rules and uncertainty, never hidden.'],
        ['Filter factor', 'Unit conversion and derivation — deterministic arithmetic over registered factors.'],
        ['Stacking filters', 'Profile composition — sector overlay × jurisdiction.'],
        ['MTF curve', 'Published uncertainty — a mandatory lens property.'],
        ['The mount', 'The profile interface on the neutral core: any registered lens by any maker attaches. Proprietary mounts are the lock-in vector, so the mount is what is standardized.'],
        ['Lens certification', 'Profiles, their version supersessions, and their trust bindings are signed, registry-deposited, log-anchored objects.'],
        ['Raw vs JPEG', 'Canonical facts vs rendered views — keep the raw, because renders change (an energy-label rescale is a re-development of the raw, not new data).'],
        ['Photograph', 'A notarized as-of snapshot.'],
        ['Aperture', 'Actor access rights.'],
        ['Optical bench', 'The conformance test suite.'],
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      The load-bearing refinement: a profile is not passive glass but a
      <strong>legal measuring instrument</strong> — pattern approval at registration,
      published calibration (version and cited rules), uncertainty and decision rules (GUM;
      ILAC-G8/JCGM-106 guard-banding for boundary measurements), and periodic
      re-verification through conformance runs. A measurement near a class boundary is
      classified differently under shared-risk versus guarded decision rules per
      jurisdiction; the framework publishes which rule each lens applies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="axes" class="plate" aria-labelledby="axes-h">
    <h2 id="axes-h">Profile axes</h2>
    <p>
      Composition is three-axial: <strong>jurisdiction × sector × characteristic</strong>.
      Jurisdiction profiles bind a legal regime (EU delegated acts, CN CCC/GB, JP METI, KR
      KC, US state law, UK UKCA); sector overlays bind an industry (electronics, textiles,
      batteries, construction, tyres, packaging; measuring instruments as the OIML pilot);
      characteristic profiles attach by <em>predicate on twin facts</em> — age, material
      content, heritage status, market status — rather than by law of place or industry.
      Characteristic profiles are regulatory-triggered (CITES species content,
      cultural-goods import under EU 2019/880, art-market AML due diligence, conflict
      minerals by origin) or voluntary value profiles (provenance and grading for antiques
      and collectibles). Time predicates fire on clocks: an object becoming more than a
      century old is an applicability event handled by the dated-binding machinery,
      requiring no human declaration.
    </p>
    <p>
      A profile is a registered, versioned object carrying: profile ID; owner or regulator;
      legal basis; effective dates; a data-point list referencing the L3 registry;
      validation rules; languages; trust requirements; access policies; presentation
      requirements; and the trigger predicate. Where regions regulate the same thing
      differently (RoHS lists), they register distinct data elements harmonized to a common
      concept — a recorded divergence, not a contradiction.
    </p>
    <p>
      Profile-set growth is dated binding, not new identity: adding a jurisdiction to an
      existing model is a registry applicability event with an effective window (including
      retroactive flags); manifest history is reconstructable as of any date; a new model
      identity arises only on a technical type change.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="capability" class="plate" aria-labelledby="capability-h">
    <h2 id="capability-h">Capability classes</h2>
    <p>
      A silent power bank and a connected instrument ship under one standard. Products in
      passport scope differ enormously in what their own devices can attest, so the
      framework fixes a capability taxonomy — and requires that a profile's freshness and
      verification requirements be satisfiable by the subject's class. Demanding live
      freshness from an S0 product is an unsatisfiable profile, not a conforming one.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      columns={['Class', 'Designation', 'Capabilities', 'Notes']}
      rows={[
        ['<strong>S0</strong>', 'Silent', 'None — testimony only.', 'Fully servable without any device participation: type certificate (certifier-hosted), operator passport, custody testimonies, recycling events, Tier-A minimum.'],
        ['<strong>S1</strong>', 'Passive-auth', 'NFC chip, PUF, or IEC 61406 identity link — identity attestation without logs.', 'The tier that EN 18246 explicitly excluded from scope: a named gap in the European series.'],
        ['<strong>S2</strong>', 'Logged-contact', 'Dumps state on physical read; no communications.', 'Battery-management-system style; the service-visited vehicle is S2 in person.'],
        ['<strong>S3</strong>', 'Connected', 'Full edge segments; commit-now, reveal-later device commitments.', 'The device is a tier of the passport system, not a client; the S3 threat model (device keys, firmware chains) applies.'],
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      Consequences: the twin is testimonial by default and sensorial by exception — for
      silent objects the twin is a ledger of testimonies <em>about</em> the thing, with
      device segments one testimony class; certification bodies can be state custodians (a
      versioned certificate is the type twin); and the threat model shifts by class, from
      carrier-to-object binding and testimony fraud at S0 to firmware and channel
      compromise at S3.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="twin" class="plate" aria-labelledby="twin-h">
    <h2 id="twin-h">Passive twin → active twin</h2>
    <p>
      The deployed reality — national 2D-code identity platforms, certificate-body
      verification portals — is the <strong>passive-twin</strong> era: the twin is missing
      as an entity; what exists is a sequence of point-in-time attestations about the
      object, sampled when the object visits an attestor. Truth is episodic; staleness
      between attestations is unbounded and undetectable; trust rides entirely on attestor
      authority. The <strong>active twin</strong> shifts the truth source to
      device-committed self-reporting under device keys: truth becomes (near-)continuous,
      freshness bounded and <em>detectable</em> (<code>fresh_within</code>), and the
      binding question shifts from “who saw it” to “who attests the sensor.” The passive
      twin maps to S0–S1; the active twin to S2–S3.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the active twin makes load-bearing: measurement validity (sensor calibration in
      device registration, GUM uncertainty on self-reported values, registered units);
      derived verdicts — state of health is computed by a model, and whose model is a
      governed transform; privacy, because usage telemetry is personal data; the S3
      security model; and conformity regime, from incident-driven inspection to continuous
      conformity monitoring, with each delegated act choosing per profile whether
      point-in-time attestation or monitoring is authoritative.
    </p>
    <p>
      Coexistence rule: both paradigms live in the framework permanently — the connected
      vehicle and the silent power bank ship under one standard. Profiles declare, per data
      element, the required truth mode: attest-sampled, self-committed, or
      both-with-precedence, where precedence between a calibrated device measurement and an
      attestor observation is a metrological hierarchy question.
    </p>
    <p class="meta">
      Lineage, named for committee purposes — evolution, not revolution; each era's
      institutions survive as attestation classes: paper type-approvals and service books →
      GS1 barcodes → national 2D-code identity platforms → certificate-body digital
      verification → verifiable-credential triads (UNTP) → active twins.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="resilience" class="plate" aria-labelledby="resilience-h">
    <h2 id="resilience-h">Availability and resilience</h2>
    <p>
      Three payload tiers fix the carrier/server split — “half in code, half on server” is
      the normative design, not a workaround. <strong>Tier A</strong> is the
      carrier-embedded minimum viable passport: product ID, resolver URI, operator ID,
      status, critical safety/recall flag, validity, and multi-suite compressed signatures,
      sized to EN 18220 carriers via compressed serialization, offline-verifiable with
      pre-cached trust anchors. <strong>Tier B</strong> is the served full passport.
      <strong>Tier C</strong> is archival (EN 18221 / OAIS). Stale or offline data degrades
      explicitly — freshness verdicts — and never silently passes.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      columns={['Failure', 'Response']}
      rows={[
        ['Client offline', 'Tier A with cached trust anchors, as-of marked.'],
        ['Origin server down', 'Mirrors and caches → transparency-log inclusion proofs → Tier C archive serving.'],
        ['Resolver outage', 'Federated alternates, national mirrors, cached linksets; the carrier retains identity and resolution is reproducible.'],
        ['Host defunct', 'Registry marking, resolver redirection, hosting-handover ceremony, escrow (EN 18221 back-up providers are the special case).'],
        ['Registry outage', 'Per-version items are immutable and mirrorable; entries are log-anchored.'],
        ['Log outage', 'M-of-K quorum across independent logs.'],
        ['Trust authority outage or compromise', 'Threshold roots, rotation with continuity, revocation-reason taxonomy with windows, emergency threshold votes.'],
        ['Authority removal', 'Affected profiles quarantined; verification proceeds under other anchors, in evidentiary mode.'],
        ['Scheme-operator failure', 'IANA-style succession obligations recorded at registration; dormant-identifier continuity.'],
        ['Jurisdictional disconnection', 'Cached anchors and logs sustain verification; evidentiary mode; reconnection ceremonies.'],
        ['Long-term migration', 'Post-quantum schedules via the agility registry; format migration because serializations are renders of the core; media migration via Tier C OAIS refresh.'],
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="trust" class="plate" aria-labelledby="trust-h">
    <h2 id="trust-h">Trust, in one section</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Verify under your own anchors.</strong> Each profile's attestations chain to
      its own jurisdiction's trust framework. A verifier in one jurisdiction can verify a
      passport from another through SIGNATIF cross-recognition — jurisdictional trust lists
      feeding a globally multi-witnessed master list, with transparency logs anchoring both
      trust events and passport events — without adopting the other jurisdiction's rules.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Multi-suite signatures.</strong> The same payload may carry several
      signatures side by side — for example ECDSA, SM2, and ML-DSA — and each verifier
      checks only what its profile-scoped acceptance policy requires. Jurisdictional
      cryptographic suites are part of the profile, not the platform.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Graded verdicts.</strong> Verification returns a verdict with a coverage
      report, not a boolean; and when a passport is declared fraudulent, the reason
      determines retroactivity — prospective reasons leave earlier good-faith
      verifications valid, retroactive reasons void validity from the start.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full trust model — the registry, co-signatures, revocation readings, stamps — is
      on the <a href="/framework/trust/">trust page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Continue
    href="/specs/"
    num="04"
    title="The specification"
    desc="The framework in normative form — five parts covering scope, profiles, events, tiers, and conformance, rendered from the AsciiDoc sources."
  />
</BaseLayout>

